WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.740
Narrator: The eighth and final season

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.070
of "Game of Thrones" has finally arrived.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.870
In the episode titled "Winterfell,"

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.760
we get a ton of references
to the very first episode

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.060
of the series, which
premiered way back in 2011.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.360
Here are 13 details you might have missed

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.160
from Season 8, Episode 1.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.763
And, of course, warning:
Spoilers are coming.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.240
Right at the top of the new episode,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.623
we get new visuals
accompanying the theme song.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.640
What's probably most important to note

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.240
is that the Northern Wall
now has a giant hole,

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.380
reminding us of the season 7 finale

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.670
where the the undead, reanimated Viserion

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:35.050
obliterates the wall.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.160
We also get three new panels
showing the fall of the Wall,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.960
the Red Wedding, and
Daenerys' dragons being born.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.340
At the beginning of the
episode, we see Daenerys

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.160
and Jon Snow entering Winterfell

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.430
with a large army and two dragons.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.210
This procession mirrors
Season 1, Episode 1

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.660
when Robert Baratheon
arrived at Winterfell

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.990
to make Ned Stark Hand of the King.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.250
Not only do the two scenes look similar,

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.483
but both scenes also share
the exact same music.

00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:09.230
The boy pushing the crowd and climbing

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.290
is similar to both Bran and Arya.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.320
Speaking of Arya, just
like Season 1, Episode 1,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.270
she catches a glimpse of the Hound,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.393
but they've got a lot more history now.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.570
The boy in this scene at the
meeting of the Northern Lords

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.130
is Lord Ned Umber, who we saw last season

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.990
pledging fealty to House Stark.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:27.853
Jon Snow: Ned Umber.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:30.920
Narrator: Who is Ned?

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.650
He's the son of Smalljon Umber,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.750
who died at the Battle of the Bastards.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.740
If you watch the episode, then you know

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.200
this isn't the last we see of the boy.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.040
But before we get there, we should explain

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.180
where exactly Tormund,
Beric, and the others

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.340
are treading carefully at
the end of the episode.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.500
Well, they're actually at Last Hearth,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.140
which is south of the Wall
but north of Winterfell,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.250
and that's where the Umbers live.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.370
At Last Hearth, Beric kills
the reanimated Ned Umber

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.550
with his flaming sword,
illuminating a spiral pattern.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.160
We've seen this pattern before.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.210
Back in season 3, we saw the
body parts of dismembered

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.050
Night's Watchmen and horses.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.010
We saw the pattern again in season 5,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.000
when the Children of the
Forest created the Night King,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.220
and then again in season 7,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.750
in the cave drawings at Dragonstone.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.790
We still don't know yet its significance,

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.050
but clearly it's an important
sign of the White Walkers.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.450
In another nod to season 1, Jon and Arya's

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.320
reunion in this episode is a close mirror

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.253
of when Jon and Arya said goodbye

00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:29.886
in Season 1, Episode 2.

00:02:29.895 --> 00:02:32.040
They hug in a near-identical way,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.210
and that's when Jon gave Arya
Needle, her beloved sword.

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:38.308
In this episode, Jon notices
that Arya still has the blade.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.060
We see Jon fly a dragon
for the first time,

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.720
which is certainly momentous,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.630
but there's actually more going on here.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.650
The dragon Jon flies is Rhaegal,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.117
named after his real
father, Rhaegar Targaryen.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.080
In the season 8 trailer,
we saw this same scene,

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.640
but Jon was not in the shot.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.680
This scene is no doubt a
reference to when Jon made love

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.250
to his first love, Ygritte,
in a cave near a waterfall.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:08.890
According to the creators,
this location in season 8

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:12.210
is also where Jon would have
hunted when he was younger.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.840
Arya meets Gendry and hands
him a drawing of a new weapon

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.000
she wants him to make out of dragonglass.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.840
In the season 2 trailer, we
see Arya with a new dagger,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.700
which could be what she's
asking Gendry to make for her.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.000
In this episode, Gendry and Arya reunite.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.917
Gendry calls Arya "Lady
Stark," to which Arya replies,

00:03:27.917 --> 00:03:30.157
"Don't call me that." Gendry then replies,

00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:31.600
"As you wish, m'lady."

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.980
This is a direct mirror
of a conversation they had

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.300
in Season 2, Episode 2.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.613
Gendry: I should be calling you "m'lady."

00:03:37.613 --> 00:03:38.842
Arya: Do not call me "m'lady."

00:03:38.842 --> 00:03:40.860
Gendry: As m'lady commands.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.670
Narrator: Tyrion says
because of Jon's courage,

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.850
they have brought the greatest
army the world has ever seen.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.070
This is similar to a
phrase Mance Rayder says

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.740
back in season 3.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.590
Mance Rayder: I'm going
to light the biggest fire

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.480
the North has ever seen!

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.200
Narrator: Mance was talking
about lighting a fire

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.850
to alert the wildlings that it was

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.830
time to attack Castle Black.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.380
In the scene with Bronn,
Qyburn gives Bronn a crossbow,

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:03.580
the weapon Cersei wants Bronn

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.590
to use to kill her traitor brothers.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.560
This is significant
because you may remember

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.053
that Tyrion uses a crossbow
to kill their father,

00:04:10.053 --> 00:04:11.247
Tywin Lannister.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.960
Midway through the
episode, Sam finds Bran,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.810
and Bran says he's "waiting
for an old friend."

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.480
We find out later the old
friend he's referring to

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.110
is Jaime Lannister, who
shows up at Winterfell.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.460
Jaime pushed Bran out a
window back in season 1,

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.990
leading to the injury that
left him unable to walk.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.913
Jaime Lannister: The things I do for love.

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:34.550
Narrator: So there you have it.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:35.680
Did we miss anything?

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.083
Let us know in the comments.

